# Response headers for Cloudflare Workers static assets.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE nginx SECURITY HEADERS, AGAIN, FOR A HOST THAT CANNOT READ
# THEM. web/nginx.conf and web/nginx-security-headers.conf were written for a
# container behind nginx. Cloudflare Pages does not run nginx and does not read
# either file, so on Pages every one of those headers is simply absent unless it
# is restated here. That is the whole failure mode this file prevents: the site
# keeps serving, looks identical, and ships with no CSP.
#
# The values below are byte-for-byte the ones in nginx-security-headers.conf, and
# TestThePagesHeadersRestateEverySecurityHeaderTheNginxConfigDeclares fails if
# the two ever disagree — in either direction, including a value edited on one
# side only. While both files exist they must say the same thing.
#
# Astro copies public/ verbatim into dist/, so this lands at dist/_headers, which
# is where Cloudflare looks. It is consumed rather than served as an asset.
#
# WORKERS APPENDS, PAGES OVERRODE, AND THAT DIFFERENCE WAS A LIVE BUG.
#
# This file used to set `Cache-Control: no-cache` on /* and rely on the later,
# more specific rules replacing it. That is Pages behaviour. On Workers Static
# Assets the values are CONCATENATED, so a hashed asset was served as
#
#   Cache-Control: no-cache, public, max-age=31536000, immutable
#
# and RFC 9111 gives `no-cache` the last word: revalidate before use. Every
# immutable asset asked the origin again on every navigation. Measured on the
# live site: 11 of 11 assets returning 304, and the render-blocking stylesheet
# costing ~588ms in front of first paint on each internal navigation.
#
# So no rule may set a header that a later rule also sets. The security headers
# stay on /* because they belong on everything; Cache-Control is now stated once
# per path class, and the HTML rule is scoped to the HTML rather than to /*.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# HTML must revalidate, or a deploy is invisible until caches expire. Scoped to
# the built pages rather than /*, so it cannot leak onto a hashed asset.
#
# EVERY PAGE IS LISTED TWICE, AND BOTH SPELLINGS ARE LOAD-BEARING.
#
# astro.config.mjs sets `build.format: "file"` with `trailingSlash: "never"`, so
# a page is BUILT as features.html and SERVED as /features. `/*.html` matches the
# request path, not the file on disk -- so it caught none of the extensionless
# URLs anyone actually visits, and four of the six pages went out with no
# revalidation at all. The failure this block exists to prevent, "a deploy is
# invisible until caches expire", was happening on /features, /comparison, /docs
# and /download for as long as the rule has been here.
#
# The .html spellings stay because both resolve: a direct hit on /features.html
# is served rather than redirected, and a rule that covers only the pretty URL
# would leave the other door open.
#
# Adding a page means adding it here. That is deliberate -- broadening this to
# /* would reach /_astro/* below and turn immutable hashed assets into
# revalidate-every-time, which is the exact leak the original comment refused.
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/features
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/comparison
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/docs
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/download
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/404
  Cache-Control: no-cache
# The comparison pages are nested, so they are SERVED at /vs/<name> and built as
# vs/<name>.html. Listed one per page for the same reason as the block above: a
# `/vs/*` wildcard would revalidate correctly today and silently stop being
# checkable, because the build guard matches a rule against each built page's
# exact served path.
/vs/aitum-multistream
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/vs/obs-multi-rtmp
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/vs/restreamer
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/vs/streamelements
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/*.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Content-hashed by the build, so it can be cached hard and forever. Same
# reasoning as the /_astro/ location in nginx.conf.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Stable but NOT content-hashed: a week, so replacing a screenshot reaches
# people. Mirrors nginx.conf's 7d on woff2/png/jpg/svg/webm/mp4.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/shots/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
